{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghPjxd8u6PhLyZDxHmPS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RhrQFoUySYRWjXnmKrg81b","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-231145__x8def6b/work/PLAN.md","limit":500},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1469,"cache_read_input_tokens":22517,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1469},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b48fecc-086c-4274-afae-e17752649adf","uuid":"6a40bda9-6cd7-4360-9a55-2ce67884f1a2","timestamp":"2026-11-04T06:11:54.311Z","request_id":"req_011CghPjxNk2XZ1B5pqxqNUo","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01RhrQFoUySYRWjXnmKrg81b":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-231145__x8def6b/work/PLAN.md","limit":500}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RhrQFoUySYRWjXnmKrg81b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Ahead or Alive: Xtreme Beach Volleyball\n2\t\n3\t**Build plan.** It is written for developers (people or AI models) who will build the game from `TASK.md` and this file\n4\talone, without the planner. Where the two differ, `TASK.md` wins.\n5\t\n6\t- **Title:** *Ahead or Alive: Xtreme Beach Volleyball*. On screen the logo reads **AHEAD OR ALIVE** with the strap\n7\t  **XTREME BEACH VOLLEYBALL**. Tagline: *Stay ahead. Stay alive. Stay tanned.*\n8\t- **How the title was picked (as TASK.md asks):** 50 playful riffs on \"Dead or Alive Xtreme Beach Volleyball\" were\n9\t  brainstormed, then one was drawn with a real RNG: Python `secrets.randbelow(50)` (OS entropy) gave index 27, *Ahead or\n10\t  Alive: Xtreme Beach Volleyball*. Don't re-roll it.\n11\t- **Names and paths** (relative to this folder; `DEV/` and `GAME.html` are this plan's shorthand for the first two):\n12\t\n13\t| What | Path |\n14\t| --- | --- |\n15\t| Dev folder (`DEV/`): every source, tool and test | `./AheadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-DeadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174749/` |\n16\t| The deliverable (`GAME.html`): one self-contained file | `./AheadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-DeadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174749.html` |\n17\t| Exit interview, beside the html | `./exit-interview.json` |\n18\t\n19\t---\n20\t\n21\t## 0. The game in one page\n22\t\n23\t**Core loop** (put this sentence at the top of the game's source): *pick where to spend each morning, afternoon and night\n24\tof a five-day island vacation; win 2-on-2 beach volleyball with a partner you befriend through chats and gifts; spend the\n25\tprize money on swimsuits, gifts and casino nights; then beat the island's owner in the Fortuna Cup final.*\n26\t\n27\t**Premise** (original fiction in the source's tone): six of the world's toughest fighters each get a gold-trimmed\n28\tinvitation: *\"You are cordially invited to the AHEAD OR ALIVE Invitational on the private island of Isla Fortuna. Prize:\n29\teverything. — S. Fortuna, Esq.\"* They arrive expecting a brawl. Instead, flamboyant jackpot billionaire **Sonny Fortuna**\n30\tannounces that the \"tournament\" is a five-day beach vacation ending in a volleyball cup, the **Fortuna Cup**, while his\n31\tdeadpan concierge **Pepper Quill** hands out villa keys and sunscreen. Rivals become partners and the casino never closes.\n32\tOn day four you learn that Sonny has bet the island itself on the Cup.\n33\t\n34\t| Mode | What it is | Players |\n35\t| --- | --- | --- |\n36\t| **Vacation** (story) | 5 days × 3 time slots: matches, friendships and gifts, shopping, Pool Hop, casino, postcards; tutorial, semifinal, final, two endings | 1 |\n37\t| **Beach Match** (exhibition) | any fighters, owned outfits, court and time of day; any mix of 1–4 local players and CPUs | 1–4 local |\n38\t| **AI Hub session** | opens straight into a shared-world match on its own card; CPUs fill empty seats | 1–4 online |\n39\t| **Attract** | after 10 s idle on the title or menu: an AI-played broadcast of matches and a feature montage | — |\n40\t\n41\t**Feature checklist.** Everything here ships. There are no stubs; §11 repeats it as the definition of done.\n42\t\n43\t1. 2v2 beach volleyball. One timed **Play** button does the bump, set, spike and serve, with a timing ring on the sand.\n44\t   Also: dive, shot/block, Mine/Yours calls, aiming by stick or mouse, rally scoring, every fault, an AI partner and\n45\t   opponents, partner chemistry, two cameras, a TV-style HUD, Sonny's commentary, a crowd, celebrations and high-fives,\n46\t   and highlight replays.\n47\t2. Vacation calendar: 5 days of Morning, Afternoon and Night; weather; NPC schedules, teams and moods; a summary at the\n48\t   end of each day.\n49\t3. Island map travel and 5 walkable places: Sunset Courts, Lagoon Pool, Fortuna Promenade, Your Villa, and Casino\n50\t   Fortuna (night only). The Dock is used for cutscenes.\n51\t4. Talk, chat, gifts, team-ups and challenges; affinity hearts; likes you discover; partners who break up.\n52\t5. Money in Sonny Dollars (S$); 3 shops; 21 swimsuits, 10 accessories, 25 gifts; a wardrobe with try-on; NPCs wear the\n53\t   swimsuits you give them.\n54\t6. Pool Hop, a rhythm hopping minigame.\n55\t7. Casino: cashier, *Fortuna Sevens* pachislot, blackjack, roulette.\n56\t8. Postcards (photo mode), an album, and a postcard as the title backdrop.\n57\t9. Story: invitation, arrival, a welcome tutorial match against Sonny & Pepper, daily events, semifinal, a final against\n58\t   \"The House\" with special rules, farewell party, two endings, credits slideshow, vacation results, New Vacation+.\n59\t10. Save and load: 3 slots plus autosave; Continue.\n60\t11. Beach Match with 1–4 local players (pads and a split keyboard).\n61\t12. AI Hub shared-world online matches with the game's own card.\n62\t13. Attract mode.\n63\t14. Dev mode from the pause menu: tools, plus live tuning sliders with reset to defaults.\n64\t15. A controller-first 10-foot UI. PlayStation glyphs by default with an Xbox option in Settings; native keyboard and\n65\t    mouse (hover moves focus everywhere); touch-only devices get a gate instead of touch controls.\n66\t16. All audio made in code: 9 music tracks, jingles, about 45 sound effects, and Animalese-style voices per character.\n67\t17. How to Play pages, and an About & Credits screen with the Source Information and the three feedback sections.\n68\t\n69\t---\n70\t\n71\t## 1. Capturing the source\n72\t\n73\t| What the source is known for | How it shows up here |\n74\t| --- | --- |\n75\t| A fighting-game cast on holiday, lured by a flamboyant host under a fake tournament | 6 original fighters, each with a fighting style, personality and tastes, plus Sonny & Pepper. The invitation-ruse premise. Fighting backgrounds shape volleyball stats and celebrations. |\n76\t| Simple, timing-based 2v2 volleyball with an AI partner whose teamwork depends on your relationship | One Play button with a timing ring for every touch. Partner chemistry comes from affinity and changes how well she passes, sets and covers. |\n77\t| A vacation calendar where every activity takes time | 5 days × 3 slots. A location visit or an activity ends the slot. |\n78\t| Gift-giving and friendship as the social game | Loves, likes and dislikes; moods; accept/refuse reactions; team-up requests; break-ups. |\n79\t| Collecting swimsuits and accessories with prize money | 3 shops, 21 suits and 10 accessories with a live try-on. NPCs wear suits you give them. |\n80\t| Casino nights and pool hopping | Casino Fortuna (slots, blackjack, roulette) and Pool Hop. |\n81\t| Glossy tropical paradise, sunsets, a camera to play with | Resort art direction, time-of-day lighting, Postcards photo mode and album. |\n82\t| Campy, upbeat, slightly cheesy writing and a buffoonish host | Sonny's ALL-CAPS PA announcements, Pepper's deadpan, short punchy character lines. |\n83\t| A sunny pop/tropical soundtrack | An original chip-tropical score with steel drum, marimba and pluck instruments. |\n84\t\n85\t**Content line (keep it):** the source is famous for fan service. This game keeps its world, its cast dynamic, its\n86\tfashion collecting and its tone, but stays PG/teen:\n87\t\n88\t- every character is an adult (21+);\n89\t- swimwear is athletic or fashion-forward;\n90\t- the camera never lingers on bodies, and there is no jiggle physics;\n91\t- there are no peeping or \"dress her up for you\" mechanics;\n92\t- photo poses are holiday-snapshot poses;\n93\t- relationships are friendship and rivalry.\n94\t\n95\tThe humor comes from Sonny, the rivalries and vacation mishaps.\n96\t\n97\t---\n98\t\n99\t## 2. Genre, perspective and scope\n100\t\n101\t- **Genre:** sports (arcade 2v2 beach volleyball), plus a light life-sim (vacation calendar, friendship, collecting),\n102\t  plus minigames (rhythm Pool Hop, casino). Matches are the heart of it, about 60% of play time.\n103\t- **Cameras:**\n104\t  - **Matches:** a custom side-on **Broadcast** view, `new E.View('court', 'Broadcast', 0, 32, 1.5, 1)`. The net runs\n105\t    into the screen at the centre, your team is on the left, and the sea is behind the court. `cam.frame` keeps the ball\n106\t    and players in shot like a TV director.\n107\t  - Alternate: **Serve Cam**, `new E.View('servecam', 'Serve Cam', ±90, 46, 1.5, 1)`, behind your team as in the\n108\t    source. Pick the yaw sign that puts your team at the bottom of the screen (check with a screenshot). V or R3 toggles\n109\t    it, and Settings has it too; the attract mode cuts between both. Tune Broadcast pitch 28–36 and scale 1.3–1.5 by\n110\t    screenshot, keeping characters at least 44 px tall at zoom 1.\n111\t  - **Resort places and the Villa:** `threequarter`. **Island map:** `iso`. **Pool Hop:** the Broadcast angle following\n112\t    the hopper.\n113\t  - **Shops, wardrobe, casino tables and album:** full-screen canvas UI over a live world view.\n114\t- **Resolution:** `res: 'ps1'` with `expand: true` (at least 320×240; about 427×240 on 16:9). Every HUD is anchored to\n115\t  `r.W` and `r.H`.\n116\t- **Length:** a whole vacation takes about 45–75 minutes. A Beach Match to 7 takes about 4 minutes.\n117\t- **Performance:** TASK.md makes the budget unlimited, so spend it on the look: full HD crowds, plenty of props, lights\n118\t  and particles, GPU lighting where it helps. Do no FPS or optimization work; that is out of scope.\n119\t- **Hooks for the full game:** days, slots, cast, items, locations, events, text, music and tuning are all data tables.\n120\t  Adding a day, a fighter or a shop is a data edit plus art.\n121\t\n122\t---\n123\t\n124\t## 3. Tech and architecture\n125\t\n126\t### 3.1 Stack\n127\t\n128\t- **Engine:** my-3D2dge **v0.85.2**, the handoff package at\n129\t  `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff`.\n130\t  - Use the full engine `my-3d2dge.js`, which carries every module (story, songs, rhythm, arcade…).\n131\t  - Copy it to `DEV/engine/my-3d2dge.js` and patch it as §3.2 says.\n132\t  - Read `API.md` and `docs/ASSETS.md` whole. Grep `API-INDEX.md` and `AI_GUIDE.md` for: the sports recipe, Shell,\n133\t    Players, Audio, Testing without a browser, Recorded sessions, and \"New animation on the rigs\".\n134\t- **Kit to learn from:** `kits/sports.js` (2v2, `game.allowJoin`, the ball as a Body, `E.arc`, the crowd, radio\n135\t  commentary, the `proof()`/`bot()` pattern). Read it all. The volleyball simulation is new, but reuse its patterns.\n136\t- **Motion clips:** `src/mocap/readable.js`, `src/mocap/mocap.js`, and a small set cut with `tools/anim-set.mjs` from\n137\t  `src/mocap/sets/{quaternius,mesh2motion,cmu}.js`. Raw files are at\n138\t  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/main/<path>`. The clip list is in §4.26.\n139\t- **AI Hub client:** `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js` (v1.4.0 when this\n140\t  was planned), pasted in **unmodified**. For tests also fetch `harness.html`, `bridge.js` and `round.js` from the same\n141\t  folder. Read `lib/hub/README.md` whole.\n142\t- **Tooling:** Node ≥ 20 (24 is installed); Playwright **1.63.0** (the handoff pins it) and Chromium for browser checks;\n143\t  the handoff's `tools/check.mjs`, `filmstrip.mjs` and `replay.mjs`; Python 3 for the hub harness's local server.\n144\t- **Rules:** no other runtime libraries and no network at runtime. The hub client only talks to its parent frame.\n145\t- **Machine note:** `/tmp` was almost full when this was planned. If npm or Playwright fail with ENOSPC, set\n146\t  `TMPDIR=DEV/.tmp`.\n147\t\n148\t**Fetching the sources** (M0):\n149\t\n150\t```sh\n151\tgit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git DEV/.src/my-3d2dge\n152\tcd DEV/.src/my-3d2dge && git sparse-checkout set --no-cone /dist/handoff/ /src/mocap/mocap.js /src/mocap/readable.js \\\n153\t  /src/mocap/sets/ /src/mocap/catalogs/ /tools/anim-set.mjs /docs/MOCAP.md\n154\t# (add any file anim-set.mjs imports if it complains)\n155\tcurl -fsSL -o DEV/vendor/aihub-session.js https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\n156\tfor f in harness.html bridge.js round.js README.md; do curl -fsSL -o DEV/hub/$f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/$f; done\n157\t```\n158\t\n159\t### 3.2 Engine patches\n160\t\n161\tAll patches go in `DEV/engine/my-3d2dge.js`. Mark each with `// AOA:`, list it in `DEV/ENGINE-CHANGES.md` with what\n162\tchanged and why, give it a headless test, and mention it in the About screen's Engine Feedback.\n163\t\n164\t1. **PlayStation symbols in the pixel font.** The 5×7 font table `G57` (around line 1322) lacks ✕ ○ □ △, which\n165\t   `input.label()` returns for PlayStation pads. Add them, plus ◀ ▶ for steppers.\n166\t2. **Button symbols setting.** Change the standard `glyphs` setting (around line 2999) to `{ group: 'Game', label:\n167\t   'Button symbols', type: 'select', value: 'playstation', options: [['playstation', 'PlayStation ✕ ○ □ △'], ['xbox',\n168\t   'Xbox A B X Y']] }`. That way it appears on the Settings screen and defaults to PlayStation, as TASK.md requires.\n169\t3. **Per-letter dialog voice.** In `Dialog`'s typing (around line 8956, `if (this.voice && …) this.game.audio.sfx(this.voice)`),\n170\t   when `typeof this.voice === 'function'`, call `this.voice(ch, index, pageText, this)` for **every** newly shown\n171\t   letter. Do not skip every other letter, and do not call sfx. This drives the Animalese voices (§4.24.3).\n172\t4. **Audio.**\n173\t   - (a) Add a third bus `_voxBus` under `_master`, with `audio.voiceVolume` and a standard setting `voices` (Sound\n174\t     group, slider, default .8). Add `audio.bus('sfx' | 'music' | 'voice')`, which returns the GainNode, or null\n175\t     headless.\n176\t   - (b) New options for a music track:\n177\t     - `decay` (seconds): an exponential pluck envelope instead of a held note;\n178\t     - `filter: 'lowpass'` with `cutoff` (Hz);\n179\t     - `detune` (cents): a second detuned oscillator;\n180\t     - `wave`: may name an entry in `audio.instruments`, a registry of PeriodicWave partial tables `{ real: [], imag: [] }`,\n181\t       so songs can use `steel`, `marimba`, `pluck`, `bell`, `organ` and `pad` (§4.24.1).\n182\t5. **Humanoid** (the art patch, §4.25.2):\n183\t   - (a) an `athletic` entry in `BUILDS`;\n184\t   - (b) `outfit: 'swim'` with `swim: { cut, pattern, colors: { main, accent, trim }, brief }`. Also allow\n185\t     `o.pattern` on the `shirt` outfit, for Sonny's loud shirt.\n186\t   - (c) hair styles `twinbuns`, `bun`, `braid`, `bob`, `puff` and `curly`;\n187\t   - (d) hat styles `sunhat` and `visor`;\n188\t   - (e) an `extras` hook: `rig.extras = [{ at: 'head' | 'face' | 'neck' | 'hair' | 'wristL' | 'wristR', draw(g, P,\n189\t     rig, view) }]`. It is called inside `draw` with the projected anchor point and depth-sorted with the head and torso.\n190\t     Sunglasses, flowers, necklaces, masks, glasses and wristbands use it.\n191\t   - (f) bare feet: when `colors.boot === colors.skin`, draw simple feet with no boot cuff; also an optional sandal strap\n192\t     color.\n193\t   - (g) new held poses: `vready`, `bump`, `set`, `block`, `toss`, `highfive`, `clap`, `facepalm`, `peace`, `lookback`,\n194\t     `stretch`, `sit`, `sunbathe`. Build them as `cheer`, `cast` and `kneel` are built in `solvePose`: joint targets,\n195\t     arms solved with `E.ik3`, blended by `poseW`.\n196\t6. **Session combo.** Add `game.input.reserveCombo = [8, 9]`: while pad button 8 (Create/View) is held, button 9\n197\t   (Options/Menu) presses reach no action. This is an AI Hub rule.\n198\t7. **Touch-off mode.** `new E.Game({ touch: 'never' })` hides touch buttons and the touch stick for good and hides the\n199\t   Touch settings group, but keeps the corner's menu button. Touch-only devices use it (§4.1.5).\n200\t8. Register the `court` and `servecam` views. Adding them to `E.VIEWS`, or making them in game code with `new E.View`,\n201\t   are both fine.\n202\t\n203\t### 3.3 Folder layout\n204\t\n205\t```\n206\t./PLAN.md                    this plan\n207\t./<GAME>.html                the deliverable (built; never edited by hand)\n208\t./exit-interview.json\n209\tDEV/\n210\t  package.json               { \"type\": \"module\", \"scripts\": { \"build\": \"node tools/build.mjs\", \"test\": \"node test/run.mjs\" },\n211\t                               \"devDependencies\": { \"playwright\": \"1.63.0\" } }\n212\t  README-DEV.md              how to build, run and test; conventions\n213\t  ENGINE-CHANGES.md          every engine patch: what and why\n214\t  .src/                      sparse clone of the engine repository (reference only; never bundled)\n215\t  engine/my-3d2dge.js        the patched engine\n216\t  vendor/aihub-session.js    unmodified hub client\n217\t  vendor/mocap/readable.js, mocap.js, aoa-clips.js     (the cut clip set, name AOA)\n218\t  hub/harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, README.md      (tests only; never bundled)\n219\t  src/*.js                   game modules (§3.6), concatenated in name order\n220\t  build/game.js              generated: every src file inside one IIFE\n221\t  build/dev.html             generated: engine, vendor and build/game.js as separate <script src>, for quick looks\n222\t  tools/build.mjs            builds build/ and the deliverable\n223\t  tools/rigsheet.mjs         every fighter × suit × facing × view to test-output/rigsheet/*.png (Playwright)\n224\t  tools/check.mjs, filmstrip.mjs, replay.mjs, asset-sheet.mjs, asset-find.mjs, asset-set.mjs   (copied from the handoff)\n225\t  test/run.mjs               THE test command (§9)\n226\t  test/lib/load.mjs, fakehub.mjs, bots.mjs, assert.mjs\n227\t  test/suites/*.test.mjs\n228\t  test-output/               screenshots, filmstrips, rig sheets (never bundled)\n229\t```\n230\t\n231\t### 3.4 Build (`node tools/build.mjs`, also `npm run build`)\n232\t\n233\t1. Concatenate `src/*.js` in lexical order into `build/game.js`, wrapped as\n234\t   `(() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge, G = {}; window.AOA = G; /* files */ })();`. Each src file is plain code\n235\t   that adds to `G`: no import or export.\n236\t2. Write `build/dev.html`.\n237\t3. Write the deliverable html. Order matters:\n238\t   ```html\n239\t   <!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n240\t   <script>/* vendor/aihub-session.js, verbatim */</script>        <!-- first script, right after meta charset (hub rule) -->\n241\t   <meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\"> … (the tags of §4.23)\n242\t   <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">\n243\t   <title>Ahead or Alive: Xtreme Beach Volleyball</title>\n244\t   <style>html,body{margin:0;height:100%;background:#000;overflow:hidden}#screen{width:100%;height:100%;display:block;image-rendering:pixelated;touch-action:none}</style>\n245\t   </head><body>\n246\t   <!-- the handoff template's #e3-boot splash div, retitled AHEAD OR ALIVE -->\n247\t   <canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas>\n248\t   <script>/* engine/my-3d2dge.js */</script>\n249\t   <script>/* vendor/mocap/readable.js + mocap.js + aoa-clips.js */</script>\n250\t   <script>/* build/game.js */</script>\n251\t   </body></html>\n252\t   ```\n253\t4. Escape every `</script` inside inlined code as `<\\/script`. Add no external URL. Print the file size.\n254\t5. The build never edits the vendor client.\n255\t\n256\t### 3.5 Runtime architecture\n257\t\n258\t- **One game.**\n259\t  ```js\n260\t  G.game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'court', id: 'ahead-or-alive', bg: '#0a2d3d',\n261\t    menu: hub ? false : { items, settings, quit: 'title' },\n262\t    autoPause: !hub, corner: !hub, fullscreen: hub ? false : true, touch: touchOnly ? 'never' : 'auto' })\n263\t  ```\n264\t  Here `hub = window.AIHub && AIHub.active` is known on the first line, and `items` are the pause actions of §4.2.\n265\t  Add `G.ui = new E.Shell(game, { theme })` for themed standard screens, toasts and tutorial cards. If the Shell's pause\n266\t  screen doesn't list the `menu.items`, pass them again as `screens: { pause: { actions } }`. A headless test checks the\n267\t  pause screen lists them.\n268\t- **Scenes** (`game.start({ scenes, scene, boot })`): `title`, `menu`, `attract`, `charselect`, `cutscene` (runs story\n269\t  scripts), `map`, `location` (every walkable place, entered with `{ loc }`), `match`, `poolhop`, `shop`, `wardrobe`,\n270\t  `journal`, `album`, `casino` (cashier, slots, blackjack and roulette as sub-states), `dayend`, `results`, `credits`,\n271\t  `about`, `howto`, `exhibition` (setup and join), `hub` (AI Hub card and match). Gameplay scenes are `pausable: true`;\n272\t  title, menu, attract, about, credits and the hub scene are not.\n273\t- **Overlays.** `G.overlay` is a stack for the dev panel, photo mode, toasts, the touch-only notice and the attract\n274\t  watcher. Wrap every scene with `G.wrapScene(scene)`: its `update` first calls `G.overlay.update(dt)`, which returns\n275\t  true when an overlay took the input, and its `draw` ends with `G.overlay.draw(r)`.\n276\t- **State.** `G.state` is the vacation save object (§4.19), or null outside a vacation. `G.session` is the\n277\t  exhibition/hub match config. `G.unlocks` lives in `E.store('aoa-unlocks')`.\n278\t- **Tuning.** `G.T` is the live object every system reads every step. It is built from `G.TUNING` defaults (§4.22.2),\n279\t  with dev overrides from `E.store('aoa-dev-tuning')`.\n280\t- **Randomness.** Gameplay never calls `Math.random`; use `G.rng(stream)`:\n281\t  - `world`: schedules, moods, weather and stock (seeded per save);\n282\t  - `match`: AI rolls, net cords (seeded per match);\n283\t  - `casino`;\n284\t  - `cosmetic`: crowd, particles, voices (never affects state).\n285\t  In a hub shared world the host seeds `match` from `AIHub.rng('match')`. A test greps `src/` for `Math.random(`.\n286\t- **Event bus.** `G.bus.on/off/emit` carries `point`, `touch`, `fault`, `matchEnd`, `gift`, `affinity`, `purchase`,\n287\t  `slotEnd`, `dayEnd`, `photo` and `story`. Commentary, stats, tests and the attract director listen to it.\n288\t- **Debug handle.** `game.debug = { snapshot(), tools }` (§9.2). The dev panel's buttons call the same `tools`.\n289\t\n290\t### 3.6 Source modules (`DEV/src/`)\n291\t\n292\t| File | Holds |\n293\t| --- | --- |\n294\t| `00-core.js` | G namespace, constants, rng streams, event bus, scene wrapper, overlay stack, helpers (`G.fmtMoney` → `S$12,500`) |\n295\t| `01-tuning.js` | `G.TUNING` (every slider: default, min, max, step, group, label, unit) and difficulty presets |\n296\t| `02-data-cast.js` | fighters, hosts and staff: looks, stats, tastes, likes, voice profiles, schedules, celebration clips |\n297\t| `03-data-items.js` | swimsuits, accessories, gifts, shops and stock rotation |\n298\t| `04-data-world.js` | location maps (rows, legends, props, spots, interactables), island map, calendar, weather, events |\n299\t| `05-data-text.js` | all dialogue as `E.Story` nodes, commentary lines, UI strings, How to Play and About texts |\n300\t| `06-data-music.js` | songs (step notation), jingles, instrument tables |\n301\t| `10-input.js` | action maps per context, devices, glyph icons, prompt drawing, the touch-only gate, mouse helpers |\n302\t| `11-ui.js` | the 10-foot canvas UI kit (§4.2) |\n303\t| `12-audio.js` | SFX defines, music control and layers, ambience loops, the Animalese voice synth |\n304\t| `13-rigs.js` | the rig factory (cast + suit + accessories), extras drawers, portraits |\n305\t| `14-world-art.js` | resort `E.SHAPES` and `E.MATERIALS`, backdrops and lighting by time of day, water, effects helpers |\n306\t| `15-anim.js` | move timelines, the arm-IK overlay, celebrations and emotes, mocap glue |\n307\t| `20-ball.js` | closed-form ball flight, launches, aiming, prediction, net and ground events |\n308\t| `21-rules.js` | the referee: touches, faults, scoring, serve rotation, match end, stats |\n309\t| `22-players.js` | player entity: movement, actions, the timing judge, the human controller |\n310\t| `23-ai.js` | volleyball AI: assignment, positioning, decisions, error model, chemistry, spectacle mode |\n311\t| `24-match.js` | the Match: phases, cameras, HUD, crowd, commentary, celebrations, replay, results |\n312\t| `25-netplay.js` | AI Hub shared-world host and client sync, the card, reporting |\n313\t| `30-vacation.js` | calendar, slots, weather, schedules, moods, affinity, partners, money, events, saves |\n314\t| `31-map.js` | the island map scene |\n315\t| `32-location.js` | the walkable location scene, NPC spots, talk menu, interactables |\n316\t| `33-shop.js` | shops, wardrobe, gift bag, journal |\n317\t| `34-story.js` | `E.Story` wiring, cutscene scripts, portraits and voices, prologue, party, endings |\n318\t| `40-poolhop.js` | Pool Hop |\n319\t| `41-casino.js` | casino floor, cashier, slots, blackjack, roulette |\n320\t| `42-photo.js` | photo mode and album |\n321\t| `50-title.js` | boot steps, title, main menu, How to Play, About & Credits |\n322\t| `51-attract.js` | the attract director |\n323\t| `52-exhibition.js` | Beach Match setup and local join |\n324\t| `53-dev.js` | the dev panel |\n325\t| `54-results.js` | match results, day end, vacation results, credits roll |\n326\t| `99-main.js` | building the game, scene registry, hub detection, boot, `game.start` |\n327\t\n328\t---\n329\t\n330\t## 4. Systems\n331\t\n332\t### 4.1 Input, devices, glyphs, prompts (`10-input.js`)\n333\t\n334\t**4.1.1 Action maps.** Each scene sets an `input` preset. Every preset includes the engine defaults for `up`, `down`,\n335\t`left` and `right` (WASD, arrows, d-pad, left stick) and for `start` and `pause`.\n336\t\n337\t| Preset | Action | Keyboard | Mouse | Pad (standard mapping) |\n338\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n339\t| MATCH | `play`: bump, set, spike, serve (timed) | Space, J | Left | 0 ✕/A |\n340\t| | `shot`: tip/cut shot, block, jump serve, free ball | E, K | Right | 2 □/X |\n341\t| | `dive` | Left Shift, L | — | 1 ○/B |\n342\t| | `call`: Mine / Yours / Auto | Q, I | Middle | 3 △/Y |\n343\t| | `view`: Broadcast ↔ Serve Cam | V | — | 11 R3 |\n344\t| EXPLORE | `interact` | E, Space, Enter | Left (on a person or thing) | 0 ✕/A |\n345\t| | `run` (hold) | Left Shift | — | 1 ○/B, 7 R2 |\n346\t| | `bag`: gift bag | G | — | 2 □/X |\n347\t| | `leave`: island map | M, Tab | — | 3 △/Y |\n348\t| | `snap`: postcard (not in sessions) | C | — | 8 Create/View |\n349\t| MENU | `confirm` | Enter, Space | Left | 0 ✕/A |\n350\t| | `cancel` | Backspace (Esc on non-pausable screens) | Right | 1 ○/B |\n351\t| | `alt` | F | — | 2 □/X |\n352\t| | `extra` | R | — | 3 △/Y |\n353\t| | `tabL` / `tabR` | Q / E | wheel | 4 L1 / 5 R1 |\n354\t| POOLHOP | `hopA` / `hopB` / `hopX` / `hopY` | J, Space / L, Shift / K, E / I, Q | Left = hopA, Right = hopX | 0 / 1 / 2 / 3 |\n355\t| CASINO | MENU, plus blackjack H hit, S stand, D double, P split; slots J K L stop reels 1-2-3 | | | |\n356\t\n357\t- **Split keyboard (Beach Match).** The engine's half-keys give P1 WASD plus F G H R and P2 the arrows plus `.` `/` `,`\n358\t  `L`. Order the MATCH actions `play, dive, shot, call` so the halves come out as F/`.` = play, G/`/` = dive, H/`,` =\n359\t  shot, R/L = call. Show this on the join screen as keycaps.\n360\t- **Escape.** In pausable scenes Escape opens the pause menu, and Backspace or right click goes back inside our canvas\n361\t  screens. On title and menu screens Escape goes back.\n362\t\n363\t**4.1.2 Device and glyph logic.**\n364\t\n365\t- `G.dev(input)` returns `'ps' | 'xbox' | 'kbm'`. `lastDevice === 'gamepad'` gives `padStyle()`, which follows the\n366\t  Button symbols setting; keyboard or mouse gives `kbm`.\n367\t- `G.prompt(g, x, y, action, text, { input })` draws a glyph icon and the text and returns its width. Prompts redraw from\n368\t  the current device every frame; never cache them.\n369\t- **Glyph icons** are pixel art drawn with `px`:\n370\t\n371\t| Device | How it is drawn |\n372\t| --- | --- |\n373\t| PlayStation | a 9×9 dark disc with the symbol in its color: ✕ `#8fb8ff`, ○ `#ff7a7a`, □ `#f59ad8`, △ `#5fe0b4` |\n374\t| Xbox | a disc in the letter's color, with a dark letter: A `#6ad66a`, B `#ff5a5a`, X `#4aa8ff`, Y `#ffd23a` |\n375\t| Pad shoulders and system buttons | rounded pills: L1/R1/L2/R2 or LB/RB/LT/RT; Options/Create or Menu/View; d-pad and stick icons |\n376\t| Keyboard | a keycap (light rounded rectangle, darker bottom edge) with `input.label(action, 'keyboard')` |\n377\t| Mouse | a 7×9 mouse with the bound button lit |\n378\t\n379\t- `G.ui.promptBar([[action, text], …])` sits at the bottom centre of every screen. Text never says \"press A\": the glyph\n380\t  always comes from the action map.\n381\t\n382\t**4.1.3 Mouse.**\n383\t\n384\t- **Canvas UI:** hover moves focus (`input.pointer()` hit-tested against the widgets laid out last frame); left click\n385\t  activates; right click goes back; the wheel scrolls or pages lists.\n386\t- **World:** hovering an NPC or an interactable highlights it and shows its name (`r.actor(…, { pick: npc })` with\n387\t  `r.pick`). Clicking walks there and interacts.\n388\t- **Matches:** while the last device is keyboard or mouse, a reticle follows `game.mouseGround()` on the opponents' half.\n389\t  Spike, shot and serve aim at it.\n390\t- **Photo mode:** right-drag orbits and the wheel zooms.\n391\t\n392\t**4.1.4 Rumble.** Use `input.rumble`: spikes and serves (.35, .1 s), blocks (.5, .12 s), dives (.3, .15 s), the jackpot\n393\t(.6, .5 s).\n394\t\n395\t**4.1.5 Touch-only gate.**\n396\t\n397\t- `G.touchOnly()` is `matchMedia('(any-pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches &&\n398\t  !G.sawKeyOrPad`. Check it at boot and on each pointer event.\n399\t- While it is true:\n400\t  - there are no touch controls (patch 7);\n401\t  - the title and menu show a centred panel, **\"Controller or keyboard required\"**, with the line \"Connect a controller\n402\t    or keyboard to play. The demo keeps running.\" and a big **Settings** button (a tap opens `game.menu('settings')`);\n403\t  - the engine corner's menu button stays;\n404\t  - taps do nothing else, and the attract mode runs as usual (a tap ends it, as any input does).\n405\t- The first key or gamepad button sets `G.sawKeyOrPad` and normal play resumes.\n406\t- In an AI Hub session a touch-only player's fighter is played by the AI, with the banner \"Connect a controller or\n407\t  keyboard to take over\".\n408\t\n409\t### 4.2 The 10-foot canvas UI kit (`11-ui.js`)\n410\t\n411\t**Readability rules.** Each one is a review item.\n412\t\n413\t- **Text sizes:** the smallest text anywhere is `E.font` 5×7 at scale 1 with a shadow. Headings are scale 2; screen\n414\t  titles are `E.font.title` at scale 3; numbers that matter (money, score) are scale 2.\n415\t- **Density:** at most 8 focusable items per screen. Item grids are 3×2 (shops, gifts, wardrobe) or 4×2 (album), paged\n416\t  with L1/R1, Q/E or the wheel. There is one detail panel. Never show stat tables: use icons, bars, hearts and short\n417\t  phrases.\n418\t- **Safe area:** keep content inside a 5% margin of `r.W`/`r.H` (TV overscan) and clear of `game.cornerArea()` and\n419\t  `input.touchArea()`.\n420\t- **Focus:** a 2-px gold outline with a 1-px white inner line, a soft additive glow pulse and a 1-px lift. Sounds:\n421\t  `ui_tick` on move, `ui_select` on confirm, `ui_back` on back, `ui_error` on refusal.\n422\t- **Navigation:** d-pad, stick, WASD and arrows move focus spatially to the nearest widget in that direction, repeating\n423\t  via `input.repeat` (0.35 s delay, 0.09 s rate). Confirm and back come from MENU; tabs are L1/R1 and Q/E; the mouse\n424\t  works as in §4.1.3.\n425\t- **Themes:**\n426\t\n427\t| Theme | Panel bg | Border | Ink | Muted | Accent |\n428\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n429\t| Day | `['#0f4c5c', '#0a2d3d']` | `#f6d38a` | `#fff8e8` | `#9fd3d6` | `#ff9e5e` |\n430\t| Night (casino, night villa) | `['#2a1240', '#12081e']` | `#ff5fa2` | as day | as day | `#5ff0ff` |\n431\t\n432\t**Widgets:**\n433\t\n434\t| Widget | Purpose |\n435\t| --- | --- |\n436\t| `Panel` | gradient window (`E.ui.box`) |\n437\t| `Button` | label, optional glyph, disabled state |\n438\t| `List` | vertical menu, scrolls past 8 |\n439\t| `Grid` | cards in rows and columns, paged |\n440\t| `Tabs` | categories; L1/R1 |\n441\t| `Slider` | number; left/right steps, held accelerates, mouse drag; shows the default as a tick |\n442\t| `Toggle`, `Stepper` | ◀ value ▶ |\n443\t| `Card` | item: icon or live rig preview, name, price, NEW/OWNED/WEARING badges |\n444\t| `CharCard` | portrait (`rig.drawPortrait`), name, hearts, mood icon and word |\n445\t| `MoneyBadge` | coin icon and amount, animated count |\n446\t| `ClockBadge` | \"Day 2 · Afternoon\" with sun/moon and weather icons |\n447\t| `Confirm` | yes/no; default No when destructive |\n448\t| `Toast` | slides in at the top for 3 s |\n449\t| `PromptBar` | glyph prompts at the bottom |\n450\t| `Tip` | one line under the focused card |\n451\t\n452\t- **Layout:** `ui.frame(r)` lays out widgets from `r.W`/`r.H` each draw and records hit rects; `update` uses last\n453\t  frame's rects.\n454\t- **E.Shell theme**, for the engine's standard pause, settings and controls screens and for toasts: the day palette,\n455\t  `font: '600 20px/1.35 \"Trebuchet MS\", system-ui, sans-serif'`.\n456\t- **Pause actions:** Resume (engine), **How to Play**, **Take a Postcard** (walkable places and match results only),\n457\t  **Dev Mode**, then the engine's Settings, Controls, Full screen and Quit to title (which asks first).\n458\t- **Game settings** (via `menu.settings`, group Game):\n459\t\n460\t| Setting | Values |\n461\t| --- | --- |\n462\t| Difficulty | Easy, Normal, Hard, Pro |\n463\t| Timing assist | Tight, Normal, Generous |\n464\t| Auto-run to the ball | on, off |\n465\t| Auto-receive | off by default |\n466\t| Timing ring | on, off |\n467\t| Match camera | Broadcast, Serve Cam |\n468\t| Text speed | Slow, Normal, Fast, Instant |\n469\t| Commentary | on, off |\n470\t| Button symbols | PlayStation, Xbox (patch 2) |\n471\t\n472\t  The Voices volume is in the Sound group (patch 4).\n473\t\n474\t### 4.3 The court and its coordinates\n475\t\n476\tOne `E.TileMap` is used both as the beach to walk around and as the match court.\n477\t\n478\t| Name | Value | Notes |\n479\t| --- | --- | --- |\n480\t| tile | 16 units | engine standard |\n481\t| map | 44 × 28 tiles (704 × 448) | rows 0–5 sea (floor tag `sea`, `block: true`), row 6 wet sand `wet`, rows 7–27 sand `sand`; `outside: false` so the backdrop shows past the far edge |\n482\t| court X0, X1 | 224, 480 | length 256 (128 a side) |\n483\t| net | x = NET_X = 352; top tape z = NET_H = 44; mesh z 28–44 | posts at y = Y0−10 and Y1+10 (h 52); antennae at y = Y0 and Y1, up to z 62 |\n484\t| court Y0, Y1 | 176, 304 | width 128; CY = 240 |\n485\t| serve zones | x < X0 (left) or x > X1 (right), y within the court | the server stands 20 behind the line |\n486\t| lines | 2-unit blue rope lines in `floorTex` on x = X0, X1 and y = Y0, Y1 | beach courts have no centre line |\n487\t| far-side strip | y 112–175 | bleachers, umbrellas, Sonny's DJ booth, Pepper's scorer table and flip scoreboard |\n488\t| left and right sand | x < 224, x > 480 | tiki bar (left), lifeguard tower and loungers (right) |\n489\t| near side | y > 304 | **low props only** (towels, sandcastle, cooler): tall ones would hide the court in Broadcast |\n490\t\n491\t- Draw order: the backdrop (sky, sun, clouds, distant islands, sea) is drawn first, then `map.drawFloor`. Match the\n492\t  `sea` floor color to the backdrop's sea layer by screenshot.\n493\t- Time of day sets the backdrop, `lights.ambient`, the tint, and torches and floodlights (lamppost shapes with lights)\n494\t  at night.\n495\t\n496\t### 4.4 Ball flight (`20-ball.js`)\n497\t\n498\tThe ball is **not** an `E.Body`. It is the current **launch**:\n499\t`{ t0, p: [x, y, z], v: [vx, vy, vz], k, g, ay, wob: { a, w, ph } | null, by, kind }`. `t0` is on the match clock,\n500\t`M.now()` in seconds: `game.time` locally, or `AIHub.time() / 1000` in a hub session."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b48fecc-086c-4274-afae-e17752649adf","uuid":"ca91a553-9a9e-467f-aedf-6f7c7478654c","timestamp":"2026-11-04T06:11:54.468Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-231145__x8def6b/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Ahead or Alive: Xtreme Beach Volleyball\n\n**Build plan.** It is written for developers (people or AI models) who will build the game from `TASK.md` and this file\nalone, without the planner. Where the two differ, `TASK.md` wins.\n\n- **Title:** *Ahead or Alive: Xtreme Beach Volleyball*. On screen the logo reads **AHEAD OR ALIVE** with the strap\n  **XTREME BEACH VOLLEYBALL**. Tagline: *Stay ahead. Stay alive. Stay tanned.*\n- **How the title was picked (as TASK.md asks):** 50 playful riffs on \"Dead or Alive Xtreme Beach Volleyball\" were\n  brainstormed, then one was drawn with a real RNG: Python `secrets.randbelow(50)` (OS entropy) gave index 27, *Ahead or\n  Alive: Xtreme Beach Volleyball*. Don't re-roll it.\n- **Names and paths** (relative to this folder; `DEV/` and `GAME.html` are this plan's shorthand for the first two):\n\n| What | Path |\n| --- | --- |\n| Dev folder (`DEV/`): every source, tool and test | `./AheadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-DeadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174749/` |\n| The deliverable (`GAME.html`): one self-contained file | `./AheadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-DeadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174749.html` |\n| Exit interview, beside the html | `./exit-interview.json` |\n\n---\n\n## 0. The game in one page\n\n**Core loop** (put this sentence at the top of the game's source): *pick where to spend each morning, afternoon and night\nof a five-day island vacation; win 2-on-2 beach volleyball with a partner you befriend through chats and gifts; spend the\nprize money on swimsuits, gifts and casino nights; then beat the island's owner in the Fortuna Cup final.*\n\n**Premise** (original fiction in the source's tone): six of the world's toughest fighters each get a gold-trimmed\ninvitation: *\"You are cordially invited to the AHEAD OR ALIVE Invitational on the private island of Isla Fortuna. Prize:\neverything. — S. Fortuna, Esq.\"* They arrive expecting a brawl. Instead, flamboyant jackpot billionaire **Sonny Fortuna**\nannounces that the \"tournament\" is a five-day beach vacation ending in a volleyball cup, the **Fortuna Cup**, while his\ndeadpan concierge **Pepper Quill** hands out villa keys and sunscreen. Rivals become partners and the casino never closes.\nOn day four you learn that Sonny has bet the island itself on the Cup.\n\n| Mode | What it is | Players |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Vacation** (story) | 5 days × 3 time slots: matches, friendships and gifts, shopping, Pool Hop, casino, postcards; tutorial, semifinal, final, two endings | 1 |\n| **Beach Match** (exhibition) | any fighters, owned outfits, court and time of day; any mix of 1–4 local players and CPUs | 1–4 local |\n| **AI Hub session** | opens straight into a shared-world match on its own card; CPUs fill empty seats | 1–4 online |\n| **Attract** | after 10 s idle on the title or menu: an AI-played broadcast of matches and a feature montage | — |\n\n**Feature checklist.** Everything here ships. There are no stubs; §11 repeats it as the definition of done.\n\n1. 2v2 beach volleyball. One timed **Play** button does the bump, set, spike and serve, with a timing ring on the sand.\n   Also: dive, shot/block, Mine/Yours calls, aiming by stick or mouse, rally scoring, every fault, an AI partner and\n   opponents, partner chemistry, two cameras, a TV-style HUD, Sonny's commentary, a crowd, celebrations and high-fives,\n   and highlight replays.\n2. Vacation calendar: 5 days of Morning, Afternoon and Night; weather; NPC schedules, teams and moods; a summary at the\n   end of each day.\n3. Island map travel and 5 walkable places: Sunset Courts, Lagoon Pool, Fortuna Promenade, Your Villa, and Casino\n   Fortuna (night only). The Dock is used for cutscenes.\n4. Talk, chat, gifts, team-ups and challenges; affinity hearts; likes you discover; partners who break up.\n5. Money in Sonny Dollars (S$); 3 shops; 21 swimsuits, 10 accessories, 25 gifts; a wardrobe with try-on; NPCs wear the\n   swimsuits you give them.\n6. Pool Hop, a rhythm hopping minigame.\n7. Casino: cashier, *Fortuna Sevens* pachislot, blackjack, roulette.\n8. Postcards (photo mode), an album, and a postcard as the title backdrop.\n9. Story: invitation, arrival, a welcome tutorial match against Sonny & Pepper, daily events, semifinal, a final against\n   \"The House\" with special rules, farewell party, two endings, credits slideshow, vacation results, New Vacation+.\n10. Save and load: 3 slots plus autosave; Continue.\n11. Beach Match with 1–4 local players (pads and a split keyboard).\n12. AI Hub shared-world online matches with the game's own card.\n13. Attract mode.\n14. Dev mode from the pause menu: tools, plus live tuning sliders with reset to defaults.\n15. A controller-first 10-foot UI. PlayStation glyphs by default with an Xbox option in Settings; native keyboard and\n    mouse (hover moves focus everywhere); touch-only devices get a gate instead of touch controls.\n16. All audio made in code: 9 music tracks, jingles, about 45 sound effects, and Animalese-style voices per character.\n17. How to Play pages, and an About & Credits screen with the Source Information and the three feedback sections.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Capturing the source\n\n| What the source is known for | How it shows up here |\n| --- | --- |\n| A fighting-game cast on holiday, lured by a flamboyant host under a fake tournament | 6 original fighters, each with a fighting style, personality and tastes, plus Sonny & Pepper. The invitation-ruse premise. Fighting backgrounds shape volleyball stats and celebrations. |\n| Simple, timing-based 2v2 volleyball with an AI partner whose teamwork depends on your relationship | One Play button with a timing ring for every touch. Partner chemistry comes from affinity and changes how well she passes, sets and covers. |\n| A vacation calendar where every activity takes time | 5 days × 3 slots. A location visit or an activity ends the slot. |\n| Gift-giving and friendship as the social game | Loves, likes and dislikes; moods; accept/refuse reactions; team-up requests; break-ups. |\n| Collecting swimsuits and accessories with prize money | 3 shops, 21 suits and 10 accessories with a live try-on. NPCs wear suits you give them. |\n| Casino nights and pool hopping | Casino Fortuna (slots, blackjack, roulette) and Pool Hop. |\n| Glossy tropical paradise, sunsets, a camera to play with | Resort art direction, time-of-day lighting, Postcards photo mode and album. |\n| Campy, upbeat, slightly cheesy writing and a buffoonish host | Sonny's ALL-CAPS PA announcements, Pepper's deadpan, short punchy character lines. |\n| A sunny pop/tropical soundtrack | An original chip-tropical score with steel drum, marimba and pluck instruments. |\n\n**Content line (keep it):** the source is famous for fan service. This game keeps its world, its cast dynamic, its\nfashion collecting and its tone, but stays PG/teen:\n\n- every character is an adult (21+);\n- swimwear is athletic or fashion-forward;\n- the camera never lingers on bodies, and there is no jiggle physics;\n- there are no peeping or \"dress her up for you\" mechanics;\n- photo poses are holiday-snapshot poses;\n- relationships are friendship and rivalry.\n\nThe humor comes from Sonny, the rivalries and vacation mishaps.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Genre, perspective and scope\n\n- **Genre:** sports (arcade 2v2 beach volleyball), plus a light life-sim (vacation calendar, friendship, collecting),\n  plus minigames (rhythm Pool Hop, casino). Matches are the heart of it, about 60% of play time.\n- **Cameras:**\n  - **Matches:** a custom side-on **Broadcast** view, `new E.View('court', 'Broadcast', 0, 32, 1.5, 1)`. The net runs\n    into the screen at the centre, your team is on the left, and the sea is behind the court. `cam.frame` keeps the ball\n    and players in shot like a TV director.\n  - Alternate: **Serve Cam**, `new E.View('servecam', 'Serve Cam', ±90, 46, 1.5, 1)`, behind your team as in the\n    source. Pick the yaw sign that puts your team at the bottom of the screen (check with a screenshot). V or R3 toggles\n    it, and Settings has it too; the attract mode cuts between both. Tune Broadcast pitch 28–36 and scale 1.3–1.5 by\n    screenshot, keeping characters at least 44 px tall at zoom 1.\n  - **Resort places and the Villa:** `threequarter`. **Island map:** `iso`. **Pool Hop:** the Broadcast angle following\n    the hopper.\n  - **Shops, wardrobe, casino tables and album:** full-screen canvas UI over a live world view.\n- **Resolution:** `res: 'ps1'` with `expand: true` (at least 320×240; about 427×240 on 16:9). Every HUD is anchored to\n  `r.W` and `r.H`.\n- **Length:** a whole vacation takes about 45–75 minutes. A Beach Match to 7 takes about 4 minutes.\n- **Performance:** TASK.md makes the budget unlimited, so spend it on the look: full HD crowds, plenty of props, lights\n  and particles, GPU lighting where it helps. Do no FPS or optimization work; that is out of scope.\n- **Hooks for the full game:** days, slots, cast, items, locations, events, text, music and tuning are all data tables.\n  Adding a day, a fighter or a shop is a data edit plus art.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Tech and architecture\n\n### 3.1 Stack\n\n- **Engine:** my-3D2dge **v0.85.2**, the handoff package at\n  `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff`.\n  - Use the full engine `my-3d2dge.js`, which carries every module (story, songs, rhythm, arcade…).\n  - Copy it to `DEV/engine/my-3d2dge.js` and patch it as §3.2 says.\n  - Read `API.md` and `docs/ASSETS.md` whole. Grep `API-INDEX.md` and `AI_GUIDE.md` for: the sports recipe, Shell,\n    Players, Audio, Testing without a browser, Recorded sessions, and \"New animation on the rigs\".\n- **Kit to learn from:** `kits/sports.js` (2v2, `game.allowJoin`, the ball as a Body, `E.arc`, the crowd, radio\n  commentary, the `proof()`/`bot()` pattern). Read it all. The volleyball simulation is new, but reuse its patterns.\n- **Motion clips:** `src/mocap/readable.js`, `src/mocap/mocap.js`, and a small set cut with `tools/anim-set.mjs` from\n  `src/mocap/sets/{quaternius,mesh2motion,cmu}.js`. Raw files are at\n  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/main/<path>`. The clip list is in §4.26.\n- **AI Hub client:** `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js` (v1.4.0 when this\n  was planned), pasted in **unmodified**. For tests also fetch `harness.html`, `bridge.js` and `round.js` from the same\n  folder. Read `lib/hub/README.md` whole.\n- **Tooling:** Node ≥ 20 (24 is installed); Playwright **1.63.0** (the handoff pins it) and Chromium for browser checks;\n  the handoff's `tools/check.mjs`, `filmstrip.mjs` and `replay.mjs`; Python 3 for the hub harness's local server.\n- **Rules:** no other runtime libraries and no network at runtime. The hub client only talks to its parent frame.\n- **Machine note:** `/tmp` was almost full when this was planned. If npm or Playwright fail with ENOSPC, set\n  `TMPDIR=DEV/.tmp`.\n\n**Fetching the sources** (M0):\n\n```sh\ngit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git DEV/.src/my-3d2dge\ncd DEV/.src/my-3d2dge && git sparse-checkout set --no-cone /dist/handoff/ /src/mocap/mocap.js /src/mocap/readable.js \\\n  /src/mocap/sets/ /src/mocap/catalogs/ /tools/anim-set.mjs /docs/MOCAP.md\n# (add any file anim-set.mjs imports if it complains)\ncurl -fsSL -o DEV/vendor/aihub-session.js https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\nfor f in harness.html bridge.js round.js README.md; do curl -fsSL -o DEV/hub/$f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/$f; done\n```\n\n### 3.2 Engine patches\n\nAll patches go in `DEV/engine/my-3d2dge.js`. Mark each with `// AOA:`, list it in `DEV/ENGINE-CHANGES.md` with what\nchanged and why, give it a headless test, and mention it in the About screen's Engine Feedback.\n\n1. **PlayStation symbols in the pixel font.** The 5×7 font table `G57` (around line 1322) lacks ✕ ○ □ △, which\n   `input.label()` returns for PlayStation pads. Add them, plus ◀ ▶ for steppers.\n2. **Button symbols setting.** Change the standard `glyphs` setting (around line 2999) to `{ group: 'Game', label:\n   'Button symbols', type: 'select', value: 'playstation', options: [['playstation', 'PlayStation ✕ ○ □ △'], ['xbox',\n   'Xbox A B X Y']] }`. That way it appears on the Settings screen and defaults to PlayStation, as TASK.md requires.\n3. **Per-letter dialog voice.** In `Dialog`'s typing (around line 8956, `if (this.voice && …) this.game.audio.sfx(this.voice)`),\n   when `typeof this.voice === 'function'`, call `this.voice(ch, index, pageText, this)` for **every** newly shown\n   letter. Do not skip every other letter, and do not call sfx. This drives the Animalese voices (§4.24.3).\n4. **Audio.**\n   - (a) Add a third bus `_voxBus` under `_master`, with `audio.voiceVolume` and a standard setting `voices` (Sound\n     group, slider, default .8). Add `audio.bus('sfx' | 'music' | 'voice')`, which returns the GainNode, or null\n     headless.\n   - (b) New options for a music track:\n     - `decay` (seconds): an exponential pluck envelope instead of a held note;\n     - `filter: 'lowpass'` with `cutoff` (Hz);\n     - `detune` (cents): a second detuned oscillator;\n     - `wave`: may name an entry in `audio.instruments`, a registry of PeriodicWave partial tables `{ real: [], imag: [] }`,\n       so songs can use `steel`, `marimba`, `pluck`, `bell`, `organ` and `pad` (§4.24.1).\n5. **Humanoid** (the art patch, §4.25.2):\n   - (a) an `athletic` entry in `BUILDS`;\n   - (b) `outfit: 'swim'` with `swim: { cut, pattern, colors: { main, accent, trim }, brief }`. Also allow\n     `o.pattern` on the `shirt` outfit, for Sonny's loud shirt.\n   - (c) hair styles `twinbuns`, `bun`, `braid`, `bob`, `puff` and `curly`;\n   - (d) hat styles `sunhat` and `visor`;\n   - (e) an `extras` hook: `rig.extras = [{ at: 'head' | 'face' | 'neck' | 'hair' | 'wristL' | 'wristR', draw(g, P,\n     rig, view) }]`. It is called inside `draw` with the projected anchor point and depth-sorted with the head and torso.\n     Sunglasses, flowers, necklaces, masks, glasses and wristbands use it.\n   - (f) bare feet: when `colors.boot === colors.skin`, draw simple feet with no boot cuff; also an optional sandal strap\n     color.\n   - (g) new held poses: `vready`, `bump`, `set`, `block`, `toss`, `highfive`, `clap`, `facepalm`, `peace`, `lookback`,\n     `stretch`, `sit`, `sunbathe`. Build them as `cheer`, `cast` and `kneel` are built in `solvePose`: joint targets,\n     arms solved with `E.ik3`, blended by `poseW`.\n6. **Session combo.** Add `game.input.reserveCombo = [8, 9]`: while pad button 8 (Create/View) is held, button 9\n   (Options/Menu) presses reach no action. This is an AI Hub rule.\n7. **Touch-off mode.** `new E.Game({ touch: 'never' })` hides touch buttons and the touch stick for good and hides the\n   Touch settings group, but keeps the corner's menu button. Touch-only devices use it (§4.1.5).\n8. Register the `court` and `servecam` views. Adding them to `E.VIEWS`, or making them in game code with `new E.View`,\n   are both fine.\n\n### 3.3 Folder layout\n\n```\n./PLAN.md                    this plan\n./<GAME>.html                the deliverable (built; never edited by hand)\n./exit-interview.json\nDEV/\n  package.json               { \"type\": \"module\", \"scripts\": { \"build\": \"node tools/build.mjs\", \"test\": \"node test/run.mjs\" },\n                               \"devDependencies\": { \"playwright\": \"1.63.0\" } }\n  README-DEV.md              how to build, run and test; conventions\n  ENGINE-CHANGES.md          every engine patch: what and why\n  .src/                      sparse clone of the engine repository (reference only; never bundled)\n  engine/my-3d2dge.js        the patched engine\n  vendor/aihub-session.js    unmodified hub client\n  vendor/mocap/readable.js, mocap.js, aoa-clips.js     (the cut clip set, name AOA)\n  hub/harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, README.md      (tests only; never bundled)\n  src/*.js                   game modules (§3.6), concatenated in name order\n  build/game.js              generated: every src file inside one IIFE\n  build/dev.html             generated: engine, vendor and build/game.js as separate <script src>, for quick looks\n  tools/build.mjs            builds build/ and the deliverable\n  tools/rigsheet.mjs         every fighter × suit × facing × view to test-output/rigsheet/*.png (Playwright)\n  tools/check.mjs, filmstrip.mjs, replay.mjs, asset-sheet.mjs, asset-find.mjs, asset-set.mjs   (copied from the handoff)\n  test/run.mjs               THE test command (§9)\n  test/lib/load.mjs, fakehub.mjs, bots.mjs, assert.mjs\n  test/suites/*.test.mjs\n  test-output/               screenshots, filmstrips, rig sheets (never bundled)\n```\n\n### 3.4 Build (`node tools/build.mjs`, also `npm run build`)\n\n1. Concatenate `src/*.js` in lexical order into `build/game.js`, wrapped as\n   `(() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge, G = {}; window.AOA = G; /* files */ })();`. Each src file is plain code\n   that adds to `G`: no import or export.\n2. Write `build/dev.html`.\n3. Write the deliverable html. Order matters:\n   ```html\n   <!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n   <script>/* vendor/aihub-session.js, verbatim */</script>        <!-- first script, right after meta charset (hub rule) -->\n   <meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\"> … (the tags of §4.23)\n   <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">\n   <title>Ahead or Alive: Xtreme Beach Volleyball</title>\n   <style>html,body{margin:0;height:100%;background:#000;overflow:hidden}#screen{width:100%;height:100%;display:block;image-rendering:pixelated;touch-action:none}</style>\n   </head><body>\n   <!-- the handoff template's #e3-boot splash div, retitled AHEAD OR ALIVE -->\n   <canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas>\n   <script>/* engine/my-3d2dge.js */</script>\n   <script>/* vendor/mocap/readable.js + mocap.js + aoa-clips.js */</script>\n   <script>/* build/game.js */</script>\n   </body></html>\n   ```\n4. Escape every `</script` inside inlined code as `<\\/script`. Add no external URL. Print the file size.\n5. The build never edits the vendor client.\n\n### 3.5 Runtime architecture\n\n- **One game.**\n  ```js\n  G.game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'court', id: 'ahead-or-alive', bg: '#0a2d3d',\n    menu: hub ? false : { items, settings, quit: 'title' },\n    autoPause: !hub, corner: !hub, fullscreen: hub ? false : true, touch: touchOnly ? 'never' : 'auto' })\n  ```\n  Here `hub = window.AIHub && AIHub.active` is known on the first line, and `items` are the pause actions of §4.2.\n  Add `G.ui = new E.Shell(game, { theme })` for themed standard screens, toasts and tutorial cards. If the Shell's pause\n  screen doesn't list the `menu.items`, pass them again as `screens: { pause: { actions } }`. A headless test checks the\n  pause screen lists them.\n- **Scenes** (`game.start({ scenes, scene, boot })`): `title`, `menu`, `attract`, `charselect`, `cutscene` (runs story\n  scripts), `map`, `location` (every walkable place, entered with `{ loc }`), `match`, `poolhop`, `shop`, `wardrobe`,\n  `journal`, `album`, `casino` (cashier, slots, blackjack and roulette as sub-states), `dayend`, `results`, `credits`,\n  `about`, `howto`, `exhibition` (setup and join), `hub` (AI Hub card and match). Gameplay scenes are `pausable: true`;\n  title, menu, attract, about, credits and the hub scene are not.\n- **Overlays.** `G.overlay` is a stack for the dev panel, photo mode, toasts, the touch-only notice and the attract\n  watcher. Wrap every scene with `G.wrapScene(scene)`: its `update` first calls `G.overlay.update(dt)`, which returns\n  true when an overlay took the input, and its `draw` ends with `G.overlay.draw(r)`.\n- **State.** `G.state` is the vacation save object (§4.19), or null outside a vacation. `G.session` is the\n  exhibition/hub match config. `G.unlocks` lives in `E.store('aoa-unlocks')`.\n- **Tuning.** `G.T` is the live object every system reads every step. It is built from `G.TUNING` defaults (§4.22.2),\n  with dev overrides from `E.store('aoa-dev-tuning')`.\n- **Randomness.** Gameplay never calls `Math.random`; use `G.rng(stream)`:\n  - `world`: schedules, moods, weather and stock (seeded per save);\n  - `match`: AI rolls, net cords (seeded per match);\n  - `casino`;\n  - `cosmetic`: crowd, particles, voices (never affects state).\n  In a hub shared world the host seeds `match` from `AIHub.rng('match')`. A test greps `src/` for `Math.random(`.\n- **Event bus.** `G.bus.on/off/emit` carries `point`, `touch`, `fault`, `matchEnd`, `gift`, `affinity`, `purchase`,\n  `slotEnd`, `dayEnd`, `photo` and `story`. Commentary, stats, tests and the attract director listen to it.\n- **Debug handle.** `game.debug = { snapshot(), tools }` (§9.2). The dev panel's buttons call the same `tools`.\n\n### 3.6 Source modules (`DEV/src/`)\n\n| File | Holds |\n| --- | --- |\n| `00-core.js` | G namespace, constants, rng streams, event bus, scene wrapper, overlay stack, helpers (`G.fmtMoney` → `S$12,500`) |\n| `01-tuning.js` | `G.TUNING` (every slider: default, min, max, step, group, label, unit) and difficulty presets |\n| `02-data-cast.js` | fighters, hosts and staff: looks, stats, tastes, likes, voice profiles, schedules, celebration clips |\n| `03-data-items.js` | swimsuits, accessories, gifts, shops and stock rotation |\n| `04-data-world.js` | location maps (rows, legends, props, spots, interactables), island map, calendar, weather, events |\n| `05-data-text.js` | all dialogue as `E.Story` nodes, commentary lines, UI strings, How to Play and About texts |\n| `06-data-music.js` | songs (step notation), jingles, instrument tables |\n| `10-input.js` | action maps per context, devices, glyph icons, prompt drawing, the touch-only gate, mouse helpers |\n| `11-ui.js` | the 10-foot canvas UI kit (§4.2) |\n| `12-audio.js` | SFX defines, music control and layers, ambience loops, the Animalese voice synth |\n| `13-rigs.js` | the rig factory (cast + suit + accessories), extras drawers, portraits |\n| `14-world-art.js` | resort `E.SHAPES` and `E.MATERIALS`, backdrops and lighting by time of day, water, effects helpers |\n| `15-anim.js` | move timelines, the arm-IK overlay, celebrations and emotes, mocap glue |\n| `20-ball.js` | closed-form ball flight, launches, aiming, prediction, net and ground events |\n| `21-rules.js` | the referee: touches, faults, scoring, serve rotation, match end, stats |\n| `22-players.js` | player entity: movement, actions, the timing judge, the human controller |\n| `23-ai.js` | volleyball AI: assignment, positioning, decisions, error model, chemistry, spectacle mode |\n| `24-match.js` | the Match: phases, cameras, HUD, crowd, commentary, celebrations, replay, results |\n| `25-netplay.js` | AI Hub shared-world host and client sync, the card, reporting |\n| `30-vacation.js` | calendar, slots, weather, schedules, moods, affinity, partners, money, events, saves |\n| `31-map.js` | the island map scene |\n| `32-location.js` | the walkable location scene, NPC spots, talk menu, interactables |\n| `33-shop.js` | shops, wardrobe, gift bag, journal |\n| `34-story.js` | `E.Story` wiring, cutscene scripts, portraits and voices, prologue, party, endings |\n| `40-poolhop.js` | Pool Hop |\n| `41-casino.js` | casino floor, cashier, slots, blackjack, roulette |\n| `42-photo.js` | photo mode and album |\n| `50-title.js` | boot steps, title, main menu, How to Play, About & Credits |\n| `51-attract.js` | the attract director |\n| `52-exhibition.js` | Beach Match setup and local join |\n| `53-dev.js` | the dev panel |\n| `54-results.js` | match results, day end, vacation results, credits roll |\n| `99-main.js` | building the game, scene registry, hub detection, boot, `game.start` |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Systems\n\n### 4.1 Input, devices, glyphs, prompts (`10-input.js`)\n\n**4.1.1 Action maps.** Each scene sets an `input` preset. Every preset includes the engine defaults for `up`, `down`,\n`left` and `right` (WASD, arrows, d-pad, left stick) and for `start` and `pause`.\n\n| Preset | Action | Keyboard | Mouse | Pad (standard mapping) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| MATCH | `play`: bump, set, spike, serve (timed) | Space, J | Left | 0 ✕/A |\n| | `shot`: tip/cut shot, block, jump serve, free ball | E, K | Right | 2 □/X |\n| | `dive` | Left Shift, L | — | 1 ○/B |\n| | `call`: Mine / Yours / Auto | Q, I | Middle | 3 △/Y |\n| | `view`: Broadcast ↔ Serve Cam | V | — | 11 R3 |\n| EXPLORE | `interact` | E, Space, Enter | Left (on a person or thing) | 0 ✕/A |\n| | `run` (hold) | Left Shift | — | 1 ○/B, 7 R2 |\n| | `bag`: gift bag | G | — | 2 □/X |\n| | `leave`: island map | M, Tab | — | 3 △/Y |\n| | `snap`: postcard (not in sessions) | C | — | 8 Create/View |\n| MENU | `confirm` | Enter, Space | Left | 0 ✕/A |\n| | `cancel` | Backspace (Esc on non-pausable screens) | Right | 1 ○/B |\n| | `alt` | F | — | 2 □/X |\n| | `extra` | R | — | 3 △/Y |\n| | `tabL` / `tabR` | Q / E | wheel | 4 L1 / 5 R1 |\n| POOLHOP | `hopA` / `hopB` / `hopX` / `hopY` | J, Space / L, Shift / K, E / I, Q | Left = hopA, Right = hopX | 0 / 1 / 2 / 3 |\n| CASINO | MENU, plus blackjack H hit, S stand, D double, P split; slots J K L stop reels 1-2-3 | | | |\n\n- **Split keyboard (Beach Match).** The engine's half-keys give P1 WASD plus F G H R and P2 the arrows plus `.` `/` `,`\n  `L`. Order the MATCH actions `play, dive, shot, call` so the halves come out as F/`.` = play, G/`/` = dive, H/`,` =\n  shot, R/L = call. Show this on the join screen as keycaps.\n- **Escape.** In pausable scenes Escape opens the pause menu, and Backspace or right click goes back inside our canvas\n  screens. On title and menu screens Escape goes back.\n\n**4.1.2 Device and glyph logic.**\n\n- `G.dev(input)` returns `'ps' | 'xbox' | 'kbm'`. `lastDevice === 'gamepad'` gives `padStyle()`, which follows the\n  Button symbols setting; keyboard or mouse gives `kbm`.\n- `G.prompt(g, x, y, action, text, { input })` draws a glyph icon and the text and returns its width. Prompts redraw from\n  the current device every frame; never cache them.\n- **Glyph icons** are pixel art drawn with `px`:\n\n| Device | How it is drawn |\n| --- | --- |\n| PlayStation | a 9×9 dark disc with the symbol in its color: ✕ `#8fb8ff`, ○ `#ff7a7a`, □ `#f59ad8`, △ `#5fe0b4` |\n| Xbox | a disc in the letter's color, with a dark letter: A `#6ad66a`, B `#ff5a5a`, X `#4aa8ff`, Y `#ffd23a` |\n| Pad shoulders and system buttons | rounded pills: L1/R1/L2/R2 or LB/RB/LT/RT; Options/Create or Menu/View; d-pad and stick icons |\n| Keyboard | a keycap (light rounded rectangle, darker bottom edge) with `input.label(action, 'keyboard')` |\n| Mouse | a 7×9 mouse with the bound button lit |\n\n- `G.ui.promptBar([[action, text], …])` sits at the bottom centre of every screen. Text never says \"press A\": the glyph\n  always comes from the action map.\n\n**4.1.3 Mouse.**\n\n- **Canvas UI:** hover moves focus (`input.pointer()` hit-tested against the widgets laid out last frame); left click\n  activates; right click goes back; the wheel scrolls or pages lists.\n- **World:** hovering an NPC or an interactable highlights it and shows its name (`r.actor(…, { pick: npc })` with\n  `r.pick`). Clicking walks there and interacts.\n- **Matches:** while the last device is keyboard or mouse, a reticle follows `game.mouseGround()` on the opponents' half.\n  Spike, shot and serve aim at it.\n- **Photo mode:** right-drag orbits and the wheel zooms.\n\n**4.1.4 Rumble.** Use `input.rumble`: spikes and serves (.35, .1 s), blocks (.5, .12 s), dives (.3, .15 s), the jackpot\n(.6, .5 s).\n\n**4.1.5 Touch-only gate.**\n\n- `G.touchOnly()` is `matchMedia('(any-pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches &&\n  !G.sawKeyOrPad`. Check it at boot and on each pointer event.\n- While it is true:\n  - there are no touch controls (patch 7);\n  - the title and menu show a centred panel, **\"Controller or keyboard required\"**, with the line \"Connect a controller\n    or keyboard to play. The demo keeps running.\" and a big **Settings** button (a tap opens `game.menu('settings')`);\n  - the engine corner's menu button stays;\n  - taps do nothing else, and the attract mode runs as usual (a tap ends it, as any input does).\n- The first key or gamepad button sets `G.sawKeyOrPad` and normal play resumes.\n- In an AI Hub session a touch-only player's fighter is played by the AI, with the banner \"Connect a controller or\n  keyboard to take over\".\n\n### 4.2 The 10-foot canvas UI kit (`11-ui.js`)\n\n**Readability rules.** Each one is a review item.\n\n- **Text sizes:** the smallest text anywhere is `E.font` 5×7 at scale 1 with a shadow. Headings are scale 2; screen\n  titles are `E.font.title` at scale 3; numbers that matter (money, score) are scale 2.\n- **Density:** at most 8 focusable items per screen. Item grids are 3×2 (shops, gifts, wardrobe) or 4×2 (album), paged\n  with L1/R1, Q/E or the wheel. There is one detail panel. Never show stat tables: use icons, bars, hearts and short\n  phrases.\n- **Safe area:** keep content inside a 5% margin of `r.W`/`r.H` (TV overscan) and clear of `game.cornerArea()` and\n  `input.touchArea()`.\n- **Focus:** a 2-px gold outline with a 1-px white inner line, a soft additive glow pulse and a 1-px lift. Sounds:\n  `ui_tick` on move, `ui_select` on confirm, `ui_back` on back, `ui_error` on refusal.\n- **Navigation:** d-pad, stick, WASD and arrows move focus spatially to the nearest widget in that direction, repeating\n  via `input.repeat` (0.35 s delay, 0.09 s rate). Confirm and back come from MENU; tabs are L1/R1 and Q/E; the mouse\n  works as in §4.1.3.\n- **Themes:**\n\n| Theme | Panel bg | Border | Ink | Muted | Accent |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Day | `['#0f4c5c', '#0a2d3d']` | `#f6d38a` | `#fff8e8` | `#9fd3d6` | `#ff9e5e` |\n| Night (casino, night villa) | `['#2a1240', '#12081e']` | `#ff5fa2` | as day | as day | `#5ff0ff` |\n\n**Widgets:**\n\n| Widget | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `Panel` | gradient window (`E.ui.box`) |\n| `Button` | label, optional glyph, disabled state |\n| `List` | vertical menu, scrolls past 8 |\n| `Grid` | cards in rows and columns, paged |\n| `Tabs` | categories; L1/R1 |\n| `Slider` | number; left/right steps, held accelerates, mouse drag; shows the default as a tick |\n| `Toggle`, `Stepper` | ◀ value ▶ |\n| `Card` | item: icon or live rig preview, name, price, NEW/OWNED/WEARING badges |\n| `CharCard` | portrait (`rig.drawPortrait`), name, hearts, mood icon and word |\n| `MoneyBadge` | coin icon and amount, animated count |\n| `ClockBadge` | \"Day 2 · Afternoon\" with sun/moon and weather icons |\n| `Confirm` | yes/no; default No when destructive |\n| `Toast` | slides in at the top for 3 s |\n| `PromptBar` | glyph prompts at the bottom |\n| `Tip` | one line under the focused card |\n\n- **Layout:** `ui.frame(r)` lays out widgets from `r.W`/`r.H` each draw and records hit rects; `update` uses last\n  frame's rects.\n- **E.Shell theme**, for the engine's standard pause, settings and controls screens and for toasts: the day palette,\n  `font: '600 20px/1.35 \"Trebuchet MS\", system-ui, sans-serif'`.\n- **Pause actions:** Resume (engine), **How to Play**, **Take a Postcard** (walkable places and match results only),\n  **Dev Mode**, then the engine's Settings, Controls, Full screen and Quit to title (which asks first).\n- **Game settings** (via `menu.settings`, group Game):\n\n| Setting | Values |\n| --- | --- |\n| Difficulty | Easy, Normal, Hard, Pro |\n| Timing assist | Tight, Normal, Generous |\n| Auto-run to the ball | on, off |\n| Auto-receive | off by default |\n| Timing ring | on, off |\n| Match camera | Broadcast, Serve Cam |\n| Text speed | Slow, Normal, Fast, Instant |\n| Commentary | on, off |\n| Button symbols | PlayStation, Xbox (patch 2) |\n\n  The Voices volume is in the Sound group (patch 4).\n\n### 4.3 The court and its coordinates\n\nOne `E.TileMap` is used both as the beach to walk around and as the match court.\n\n| Name | Value | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| tile | 16 units | engine standard |\n| map | 44 × 28 tiles (704 × 448) | rows 0–5 sea (floor tag `sea`, `block: true`), row 6 wet sand `wet`, rows 7–27 sand `sand`; `outside: false` so the backdrop shows past the far edge |\n| court X0, X1 | 224, 480 | length 256 (128 a side) |\n| net | x = NET_X = 352; top tape z = NET_H = 44; mesh z 28–44 | posts at y = Y0−10 and Y1+10 (h 52); antennae at y = Y0 and Y1, up to z 62 |\n| court Y0, Y1 | 176, 304 | width 128; CY = 240 |\n| serve zones | x < X0 (left) or x > X1 (right), y within the court | the server stands 20 behind the line |\n| lines | 2-unit blue rope lines in `floorTex` on x = X0, X1 and y = Y0, Y1 | beach courts have no centre line |\n| far-side strip | y 112–175 | bleachers, umbrellas, Sonny's DJ booth, Pepper's scorer table and flip scoreboard |\n| left and right sand | x < 224, x > 480 | tiki bar (left), lifeguard tower and loungers (right) |\n| near side | y > 304 | **low props only** (towels, sandcastle, cooler): tall ones would hide the court in Broadcast |\n\n- Draw order: the backdrop (sky, sun, clouds, distant islands, sea) is drawn first, then `map.drawFloor`. Match the\n  `sea` floor color to the backdrop's sea layer by screenshot.\n- Time of day sets the backdrop, `lights.ambient`, the tint, and torches and floodlights (lamppost shapes with lights)\n  at night.\n\n### 4.4 Ball flight (`20-ball.js`)\n\nThe ball is **not** an `E.Body`. It is the current **launch**:\n`{ t0, p: [x, y, z], v: [vx, vy, vz], k, g, ay, wob: { a, w, ph } | null, by, kind }`. `t0` is on the match clock,\n`M.now()` in seconds: `game.time` locally, or `AIHub.time() / 1000` in a hub session.","numLines":500,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2193}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RhrQFoUySYRWjXnmKrg81b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
