{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghX8k1KTcvsfL9bhRjEd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The game is finished: every TASK.md feature is in it, with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs. A fresh run just now passes all 338 checks across 18 test suites. The game file (1.9 MB) is in this folder's root, and `exit-interview.json` parses with every field answered.\n\nThese are my own checks. TASK.md says not to use subagents or workflows to review, so no one else reviewed the work. Each TASK.md requirement below is backed by a check that runs the built game or reads the delivered files. Two gaps that earlier checks claimed were covered (saves not connected, Sonny's ALL-CAPS lines) were found and fixed; both are covered now.\n\n| TASK.md requirement | How it's checked |\n|---|---|\n| Package: one HTML in the root, named as required | File present and self-contained; no external scripts |\n| Title: a riff on the source's name | Page and logo title: \"Ahead or Alive: Xtreme Beach Volleyball\" |\n| Tech stack: built on the handoff engine | Built on my-3D2dge v0.85.2, with six engine changes marked in the source |\n| Art: native style plus new assets | New resort objects as engine shapes; each fighter in her own swimsuit |\n| Animation: native system plus new animations | Over 30 new moves, and 20 motion-capture clips through the engine's player; each clip is tested to play |\n| Audio: all in code, gibberish voices | No audio files; 9 songs, about 50 sound effects, a voice for every character |\n| Online: AI Hub | The hub's own harness passes in all four scenarios |\n| The full game, long enough to show everything | A scripted vacation runs from the invitation to the results through both endings: every story event, matches, casino, Pool Hop, shops, gifts, saves |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset | Opened from the real pause menu; a slider changes a value live and resets to its default |\n| Attract mode after 10 s idle | Starts on the title and on the menu, plays every montage beat, and any input exits, in headless runs and in Chromium |\n| Multiplayer on one machine | In Chromium with real keys: P2 joins with `2`, each player steers only her own fighter, and each one's hit key plays her touches |\n| Controller first, keyboard and mouse native | DualSense ✕ ○ □ △ and Options, WASD, Space, Shift, E, Esc and mouse buttons; hover moves the focus on 10 screens; prompts follow the last device used |\n| PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox in Settings | ✕○□△ by default, ABXY with the setting, keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse |\n| Not dense web-style screens | Every menu screen has 1–8 focusable items |\n| Touch-only devices | On an emulated touch phone with real taps: the notice and Settings button show, no touch controls appear, a tap doesn't start play, and the attract runs and a tap ends it |\n| Narrative tone | Sonny speaks in ALL-CAPS bursts, Pepper never exclaims, each fighter has her own lines |\n| Done: no TODOs or stubs | Every source line scanned for stub markers, every function for an empty body, the shipped file scanned too |\n| About/Credits screen and the exit interview | The four sections in TASK.md's field order, with the same text as the exit interview (checked against each other) |\n\nFrom the earlier rounds: the island map has every landmark the plan names. Rallies average 6.2 touches, inside the plan's 3–8 target, and the match test enforces that range.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 23:11:45\n- **Source:** Dead or Alive Xtreme Beach Volleyball (Team Ninja / Tecmo, Xbox, 2003)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:**\n  - Genre: sports (arcade 2v2 beach volleyball), plus a light life-sim vacation and minigames (rhythm Pool Hop; casino slots, blackjack, roulette).\n  - Matches: a side-on TV Broadcast camera, with a Serve Cam behind your team as the alternative.\n  - Places: three-quarter view. Island map: isometric. Menus and casino tables: full-screen canvas UI over the world.\n- **Tech:**\n  - my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the full engine, with six changes) and plain JavaScript in one IIFE.\n  - The engine's mocap player with a 20-clip set.\n  - AI Hub session client 1.4.0, unmodified.\n  - Tests ran on Node 24 with Playwright 1.63.0 and Chromium.\n  - One self-contained HTML file with no runtime libraries or network.\n- **Art Direction:**\n  - The engine's native PS1-era pixel art.\n  - HD rigs on a custom athletic build, with code-drawn swimsuit cuts, 14 patterns, extra hair styles and accessories.\n  - Resort objects as engine shape data, backdrops for each time of day, and a tropical palette.\n- **Animation:**\n  - The base is the engine's own rig animation: walk, run, idle, jump and held poses.\n  - An arm-IK layer drives every volleyball move and photo pose.\n  - 20 motion-capture clips from the engine's Quaternius, Mesh2Motion and CMU sets play for celebrations, gestures, reactions, dances, idles and Sonny's pratfall.\n- **Audio:**\n  - 9 songs on the engine's sequencer, with added instruments: steel drum, marimba, pluck, bell, organ, pad.\n  - 10 jingles, about 50 sound effects and ambience loops.\n  - Animalese-style voices spoken letter by letter, with a voice profile per character.\n- **Online:**\n  - AI Hub Protocol v1, shared world, 1–4 players, with the game's own card.\n  - The host runs the match, and every copy flies the ball from the same launches on the hub clock.\n  - Each player's touches are judged on their own copy and checked by the host.\n  - CPUs fill empty seats, and the host reports the result with standings, stats and an MVP.\n- **Testing:** 18 suites behind one command (`node test/run.mjs`, which prints only failures and a count; 338 checks):\n  - headless suites driving the game through its debug handle;\n  - a clause-by-clause TASK.md audit;\n  - real-browser checks with real key and touch events;\n  - a scripted whole-vacation playthrough to both endings;\n  - a Playwright browser tour, including a touch-only device;\n  - the AI Hub harness in four scenarios;\n  - screenshots and the engine's checker for looks;\n  - no subagents.\n- **Research:** read the engine handoff (API card, index, guide sections, asset card, the sports kit, and the source for the rig, Dialog and audio) and the AI Hub README and harness. The source game was described from general knowledge; no web research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 29 game issues:\n   - a leftover empty loop in the casino props;\n   - Sonny lines missing his ALL-CAPS bursts;\n   - saves that were never connected in the real game;\n   - the clip player replacing the rigs' own move code;\n   - two crashes, one from a name clash and one from a variable scoping slip;\n   - scenes drawn before they were set up, and a sound table the browser rejected;\n   - an attract mode you couldn't leave during its photo beat;\n   - 50-touch rallies, and AI skill that barely changed results;\n   - a slot machine paying 132%;\n   - a key clash on the split-keyboard join screen;\n   - missing font characters, plus framing and overlap problems.\n2. **How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?** About 8:\n   - saving, autosave and Continue doing nothing;\n   - rigs losing every volleyball move after their first clip;\n   - a crash on the title (two systems both named `G.dev`);\n   - a crash on every attack;\n   - the scene list missing at load;\n   - the menu crashing when drawn before it was set up;\n   - the music instrument error;\n   - the attract mode trapping the player.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?**\n   - The scripted whole-vacation playthrough.\n   - The AI-vs-AI match statistics: rallies average 6.2 touches, inside the plan's 3–8 target.\n   - The clause-by-clause TASK.md audit.\n   - For online play, the AI Hub harness in four scenarios.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** Single-frame title screenshots and the engine checker's view cycling. Each was useful once, but neither shows timing or motion.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** The character rig: one constructor gives a shaded, dressed, animated character that also draws its own portrait, and its pose can be adjusted afterwards. The fixed-step clock made every test repeatable.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** A little. I passed 0 as a ring's transparency, used a material pattern name that doesn't exist (`plaster`), and had to read the source to learn how joined players inherit split-keyboard keys.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The volleyball itself (the ball's flight, contact planning, the timing judge, blocks, faults, the AI and its error model) and the swimsuit drawing.\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Hooking into the character's drawing, which is one large function. Swimsuits needed an engine change to add a callback.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** Six changes, each marked `AOA:` in the source and listed in ENGINE-CHANGES.md:\n   - PlayStation symbols and stepper arrows added to the pixel font;\n   - a Button symbols setting: PlayStation by default, Xbox as the option;\n   - a per-letter voice hook in the dialog box, for the gibberish voices;\n   - a separate voice volume, plus new instrument sounds for the music;\n   - swim shorts and a swimsuit-drawing hook on the character rig;\n   - Options ignored while Create is held, an AI Hub rule.\n6. **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** A documented drawing hook for each body part, so new clothing needs no engine edit.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** It is concrete about the player's experience (10-foot controller-first UI, glyph rules, the touch-only screen, attract mode, dev sliders) and about the deliverables.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan asks for far more content than one medium-effort session can fully build, and how to trade breadth for depth was left to judgement.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** Fitting a five-day vacation sim, three minigames and online play around a timing-based sports game, with every screen controller-first.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Say which parts of the plan are musts and which are stretch goals, so the builder can scope deliberately.\n\nFiles are in this folder:\n- AheadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-DeadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-231145.html\n- exit-interview.json\n- AheadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-DeadOrAliveXtremeBeachVolleyball-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-231145/ — dev folder with the source, tests (the TASK.md audit is `test/suites/17-task.test.mjs`, the real-browser checks `test/suites/18-real.test.mjs`), README-DEV.md and ENGINE-CHANGES.md"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":831,"cache_read_input_tokens":802687,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":831},"output_tokens":701,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b48fecc-086c-4274-afae-e17752649adf","uuid":"ee80fa27-1f3d-4570-af3a-f187694ed32c","timestamp":"2026-11-04T07:49:18.360Z","request_id":"req_011CghX8jc18ogK33SY6MTtb"}
